A||N||5||mfleury||Headlight - I need your help....||||3||
Z||000000||mfleury||10-21-2001||03:36 PM||||(GT88) The left headlight will not go up or down - the motor works fine I tested already, fuse are OK as well. Any Idea??<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||WampusCat||10-21-2001||03:43 PM||||If motor runs but the light doesn't move, it's the bushings in the drive gear mechanism thingy.  The kit from Rodney Dickman is about $10 for both lights. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/retail.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/retail.html</A>  look under electical for headlight rebuild kit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mfleury||10-21-2001||03:55 PM||||The bushings is good but the motor does not spins..<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>If motor runs but the light doesn't move, it's the bushings in the drive gear mechanism thingy.  The kit from Rodney Dickman is about $10 for both lights. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/retail.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/retail.html</A>  look under electical for headlight rebuild kit.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-21-2001||05:59 PM||||Are we correct in thinking you put 12V to the motor and it ran?<P>In that case the light controller is likely bad. New these are really expencive. Try digging thru the salvage yards. SunBird with the little half door uses it. I think some Trans ams and other GM cars built for the 87 model year or later should also have it.<P>One other thought... the motors get power from 2 seperate fuse linked circuits. One of those links may have quit. You'll need to probe the connectors on the module to findout. <P>If you unfasten the left inner fender you may be able to get to it w/o removing the fender. If so, pull the wireing off and relocate the module to the inside of the fron compartment for easy future service. You'll probably have to unwrap the wires but it beats messing with the fender twice.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mfleury||10-23-2001||06:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Are we correct in thinking you put 12V to the motor and it ran?<P>In that case the light controller is likely bad. New these are really expencive. Try digging thru the salvage yards. SunBird with the little half door uses it. I think some Trans ams and other GM cars built for the 87 model year or later should also have it.<P>One other thought... the motors get power from 2 seperate fuse linked circuits. One of those links may have quit. You'll need to probe the connectors on the module to findout. <P>If you unfasten the left inner fender you may be able to get to it w/o removing the fender. If so, pull the wireing off and relocate the module to the inside of the fron compartment for easy future service. You'll probably have to unwrap the wires but it beats messing with the fender twice.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mfleury||10-23-2001||06:53 PM||||Thank you for your info. I will open the inner fender and try to get to the module. but do I replace it or repair it?? Does it look like a Relay or what... Well I beleive it is the only thing I'll find in there. TKS<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
